SWEET present gate (patch 0343, LOWCLK lever real-flush, own code; boot-arg sweetfb_gate=1, default off = behaviour unchanged): kernel half of a non-blocking glFlush. libsweetgl made glFlush a glFinish so the panel copy (AppleCLCD::present, a CPU read of the IOSurface) never sees a frame the GPU is still drawing (docs/research/33 s.3 item 4: glFinish per frame OR a GPU fence the kernel waits on). With the gate on, swap() notes the last submitted Mali seqno (sweet_mali_seq_peek, lock-free, patch 0186) and the vsync tick copies that swap only once the in-order completion watermark reached it; until then the panel keeps the previous frame, fDirty stays set and the frame is shown on the first tick after completion (never sooner than fFlushTicks after the previous copy). Fail open and loud: Mali not up/dead = no gate; a swap held back 12 ticks (200 ms) is shown anyway with `SWEET FB: GAP GATE timeout ...`. Lines: `SWEET FB: present gate ON (sweetfb_gate=1, patch 0343) ...` at start, `SWEET FB: GATE shown N defer D timeout T maxticks M` every 300 ticks while the counts move. Read-only sysctl kern.sweetfbgate = 1 tells libsweetgl (userland lowclk/real-flush) that the gate exists; without it libsweetgl keeps glFlush = glFinish. No register access, no hardware write, no new wait (the tick compares two counters). With the gate on and an unchanged libsweetgl every swap arrives complete and the gate never defers. Not covered [read]: the per-swap pixel samples of sweetfps=2 / nav probes read the surface at swap time and can see an incomplete frame when libsweetgl really flushes. Host-checked: applies after 0339, kernel builds; not run on hardware.

